From d90bff43e59368f1c922325319072e6eef556517 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 23:17:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: a setting for HDR where RGB would be 8-bit

At 3840x2160 above 30 Hz over HDMI TMDS (HDMI 2.0, and HDMI 2.1 driven
without FRL), RGB fits at 8 bits only, and i915 and amdgpu sent HDR10
and HLG at 8 bits. With linux-9966..9969 they send it in YCbCr 4:2:0 at
10 bits or more where the TV takes the mode in 4:2:0 with deep colour:
the video is 4:2:0 already, so none of it is lost, and only what is
drawn over it, the GUI and subtitles, has half the colour resolution.
The kernel does it by default; the connector property
DVBRIDGE_HDR_YCBCR420 ("deep" or "never") turns it off.

It is a choice, so Kodi offers it, at the Expert level of System >
Display, where the connector has the property (Intel and AMD; NVIDIA
chooses its format itself): "HDR where RGB would be 8-bit", "10-bit
YCbCr 4:2:0" (the default, the kernel's) or "8-bit RGB" (as before).
SetHDR() sets the property with the HDR metadata of each video, as the
setting is then (it acts only with HDR metadata, and the kernel keeps
it with the state of the connector). It applies from the next video.

The strings are English core strings (39848-39851) translated in
Estuary (31980-31983, es_es, es_ar and es_mx), as the other settings of
Hades are (CGUIUtils::GetHadesString()). DVBridgeLink.h has the names
of the kernel's properties, with a host test against the kernel header.
